Enforcement Decree of the Special Act on Strengthening the Competitiveness of and Supporting the Petrochemical Industry

Article 1 (Purpose)
The purpose of this Decree is to prescribe matters delegated by the Special Act on Strengthening the Competitiveness of and Supporting the Petrochemical Industry and matters necessary for its enforcement.

Article 2 (Scope of the Petrochemical Industry)
"Industries prescribed by Presidential Decree" in subparagraph 1 of Article 2 of the Special Act on Strengthening the Competitiveness of and Supporting the Petrochemical Industry (hereinafter referred to as the "Act") means the following industries under the Korean Standard Industrial Classification publicly notified by the Head of the National Data Agency pursuant to Article 22 (1) of the Statistics Act:
1. The following industries within the manufacture of basic chemicals:
(a) Manufacture of petrochemical-based basic chemicals;
(b) Manufacture of biomass-based basic chemicals;
(c) Manufacture of other basic organic chemicals.
2. The following industries within the manufacture of synthetic rubber and plastic materials:
(a) Manufacture of synthetic rubber;
(b) Manufacture of synthetic resins and other plastic materials;
(c) Manufacture of composite and recycled plastic materials.
3. Manufacture of biofuels and mixtures within the manufacture of other chemical products.

Article 3 (Scope of Core Strategic Petrochemical Technologies)
"Technologies prescribed by Presidential Decree" in subparagraph 2 of Article 2 of the Act means technologies falling under the following subparagraphs which the Minister of Trade and Industry determines and publicly notifies:
1. Technologies for transition to high added value for the production of highly functional chemical raw materials and materials used in the production of semiconductors, displays, batteries, transport equipment, medical devices, pharmaceuticals, etc.;
2. Technologies for reducing greenhouse gases, such as switching fuels and raw materials and recycling waste plastics;
3. Other technologies that the Minister of Trade and Industry deems necessary to strengthen the competitiveness of the petrochemical industry, such as technologies linking petroleum refining and petrochemical processes and technologies for developing substitutes for regulated chemical substances.

Article 4 (Transition to High Added Value)
"Business innovation activities in accordance with the criteria prescribed by Presidential Decree" in subparagraph 3 of Article 2 of the Act means business innovation activities for the following matters:
1. Production of products with a higher unit selling price than general-purpose products;
2. Production of products for which it is difficult to find substitute suppliers;
3. Securing technological competitiveness through such means as advancement of technological levels and differentiation of processes;
4. Improvement of the profit structure through such means as switching raw materials and innovating processes;
5. Contribution to environmental conservation, such as reduction of greenhouse gases;
6. Other matters similar to those under subparagraphs 1 through 5 which the Minister of Trade and Industry determines and publicly notifies.

Article 5 (Special Cases Concerning Authorizations and Permissions)
(1) Pursuant to Article 7 (1) 1 of the Act, the Minister of Trade and Industry may promote the following regulatory special cases or request the heads of relevant central administrative agencies to do so:
1. Where a corporation to be newly formed under the business restructuring plan of an enterprise approved for business restructuring has applied for registration of establishment and the procedure has not been completed, permitting applications for the following registrations on condition that registration of establishment is completed pursuant to that application:
(a) An application for registration of petroleum export and import business under Article 9 (1) of the Petroleum and Alternative Fuel Business Act;
(b) An application for registration of the business of producing and selling petroleum products as by-products under the proviso of Article 10 (1) of the Petroleum and Alternative Fuel Business Act.
2. Where part of the business of an enterprise approved for business restructuring is separated and transferred to a newly established corporation under its business restructuring plan, deeming the following matters concerning chemical substances used by the newly established corporation to have been registered, reported, or confirmed with the same details as for the existing enterprise approved for business restructuring:
(a) Registration, etc. of chemical substances under Article 10 of the Act on Registration and Evaluation of Chemical Substances;
(b) Confirmation of exemption from registration, etc. under Article 11 (2) of the Act on Registration and Evaluation of Chemical Substances.
3. Deferring the submission deadline where an enterprise approved for business restructuring is required to submit an amended chemical accident prevention and management plan pursuant to Article 23 (3) of the Chemicals Control Act;
4. Expedited processing of the following matters for enterprises approved for business restructuring:
(a) Confirmation of chemical substances under Article 9 of the Chemicals Control Act;
(b) Import declarations for substances acutely hazardous to humans, etc. under Article 20 of the Chemicals Control Act;
(c) Preparation and submission of chemical accident prevention and management plans under Article 23 of the Chemicals Control Act;
(d) Installation inspections of handling facilities under Article 24 (2) of the Chemicals Control Act;
(e) Business permits, etc. for hazardous chemicals under Article 28 of the Chemicals Control Act.
5. Other expedited processing, such as shortening processing periods for authorizations, permissions, etc. determined by the Minister of Trade and Industry in consultation with the heads of relevant central administrative agencies to promote business restructuring and transition to high added value by petrochemical business operators.
(2) The head of a relevant central administrative agency receiving a request under paragraph (1) shall accept it unless there is a special reason, such as concern about harm to human life, the environment, or safety. In such cases, the head shall notify the Minister of Trade and Industry of a plan of measures that includes the details and duration of application of the special cases.

Article 6 (Special Cases concerning Exceedance of Environmental Standards)
① The environmental standards for which regulatory exceptions concerning exceedance of environmental standards are to be implemented pursuant to Article 7 (1) 2 of the Act are as follows:
1. Permissible emission standards for air pollutants under Article 16 of the Clean Air Conservation Act;
2. Permissible discharge standards for water pollutants under Article 32 of the Water Environment Conservation Act;
3. Permitted emission standards under Article 8 of the Act on the Integrated Control of Pollutant-Discharging Facilities.
② The regulatory exceptions to the standards specified in the subparagraphs of paragraph (1) are as follows:
1. Deferral, until submission of a business restructuring plan under Article 9 (2) of the Special Act on the Corporate Revitalization, of the obligation under Article 26 of the Clean Air Conservation Act to install prevention facilities for emission facilities whose continued operation is uncertain as a result of business restructuring (limited to air pollutant-emitting facilities under Article 2, subparagraph 11 of the Clean Air Conservation Act that have remained continuously out of operation since before April 1, 2026). In such cases, an improvement plan under Article 21 (4) of the Enforcement Decree of the Clean Air Conservation Act shall be submitted to the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment without delay after submission of the business restructuring plan;
2. Application of the permitted emission standards in effect before the division of a corporation, where a single place of business subject to integrated control under Article 6 (1) of the Act on the Integrated Control of Pollutant-Discharging Facilities is divided into two or more as a result of the division of the corporation in the course of business restructuring, thereby requiring a change to the permitted emission standards under Article 8 of that Act: Provided, That this shall be limited to cases in which the aggregate volume of pollutant emissions after the division of the corporation does not increase compared with that before the division;
3. Other matters determined by the Minister of Trade and Industry in consultation with the heads of the relevant central administrative agencies.
③ A petrochemical business operator seeking application of the regulatory exceptions under paragraph (2) shall submit the following documents to the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment: Provided, That the documents specified in subparagraph 3 shall be submitted only when applying for the regulatory exception under paragraph (2) 2:
1. A statement explaining the necessity for and expected effects of applying the regulatory exceptions;
2. Safety management or environmental management measures associated with application of the regulatory exceptions;
3. A copy of the certificate of approval of the business restructuring plan and the relevant business restructuring plan;
4. Other documents prescribed by the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment.
④ Upon receiving an application under paragraph (3), the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment shall notify the applicant of the results of the review of the application for regulatory exceptions within 30 days: Provided, That where an additional review period is necessary for consultation with the heads of the relevant central administrative agencies, safety assessments, or similar purposes, the Minister may extend the period only once by up to 30 days.
⑤ Where a petrochemical business operator to which regulatory exceptions are applied pursuant to paragraph (4) falls under any of the following subparagraphs, the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment may revoke application of the exceptions: Provided, That application of the regulatory exceptions shall be revoked in the case specified in subparagraph 1, and subparagraph 3 shall apply only where the regulatory exception under paragraph (2) 2 has been applied:
1. Where the operator has obtained application of the regulatory exceptions by fraud or other improper means;
2. Where the operator has failed to implement the safety management or environmental management measures submitted pursuant to paragraph (3) 2;
3. Where approval of the business restructuring plan has been revoked.

Article 7 (Special Cases concerning Application of New Technologies or Conversion to New Processes)
① A petrochemical business operator seeking application of the regulatory exceptions under Article 7 (1) 3 of the Act may apply to the Minister of Trade and Industry or the head of the competent central administrative agency, as prescribed by the Industrial Convergence Promotion Act, for prompt confirmation of regulations under Article 10-2 of that Act, regulatory exceptions for demonstration under Article 10-3 of that Act, temporary permission under Article 10-6 of that Act, and certification of conformity of new industrial convergence products under Article 11 of that Act.
② The heads of the relevant central administrative agencies notified by the Minister of Trade and Industry of the details of applications under paragraph (1) pursuant to Articles 10-2 (2), 10-3 (3), and 10-6 (3) of the Industrial Convergence Promotion Act, and the heads of the competent central administrative agencies receiving applications pursuant to Article 11 (1) of that Act, may, when reviewing or examining the details of the relevant applications, consider whether application of new technologies or conversion to new processes contributes to structural advancement or an environmentally friendly transition of the petrochemical industry.

Article 8 (Period for Review of Business Combinations, etc.) Where a business combination report or any document attached thereto submitted by an enterprise seeking application of the special cases under Article 8 (1) of the Act is incomplete, the Fair Trade Commission may set a period and order supplementation of the relevant documents. In such cases, the period from the date on which the written order for supplementation is dispatched to the date on which the supplemented documents reach the Fair Trade Commission shall not be counted toward the period under Article 8 (1) of the Act.

Article 9 (Applications for Approval of Concerted Acts and Procedures, etc.)
① A person seeking approval, pursuant to Article 9 (1) and (2) of the Act, for any of the acts specified in the subparagraphs of paragraph (1) of that Article (hereinafter referred to as "concerted acts") shall select a representative business operator for the concerted acts and submit an application for approval of concerted acts in attached Form No. 1 to the Minister of Trade and Industry.
② The following documents shall be attached to the application under paragraph (1): Provided, That the documents specified in subparagraph 8 shall be attached only where the concerted acts include joint research and technological development.
1. The specific details of the concerted acts to be carried out and the methods of implementation;
2. The following materials concerning the business operators participating in the concerted acts and the state of the industry:
(a) Business reports, statements of financial position, and income statements of the participating business operators for the most recent two years;
(b) A copy of the agreement or resolution on the concerted acts;
(c) The profit and loss situation in the relevant industry and changes in the number of business operators over the most recent three years;
(d) Annual production capacity, the supply and demand situation, and changes in transaction prices at each stage of distribution for the relevant product over the most recent three years: Provided, That materials for the most recent year shall be broken down by month;
(e) The long-term supply and demand outlook for the relevant product and its major substitutes;
(f) An international comparison of the price, production efficiency, etc. of the relevant product;
3. Specific details of the industrial crisis situation under Article 9 (2) 1 of the Act;
4. The following materials concerning Article 9 (2) 2 of the Act:
(a) The status of the participating business operators' own efforts to rationalize their businesses;
(b) The reasons why the industrial crisis situation cannot be overcome through the business rationalization efforts referred to in item (a) alone;
5. The following materials concerning Article 9 (2) 3 of the Act:
(a) The specific effects achievable through the concerted acts, such as stabilizing supply chains, achieving technological innovation, and responding to changes in the environment;
(b) A comparison of the effects of the concerted acts in restricting competition with their effects in restructuring the industry or increasing efficiency;
6. Documents confirming that there will be no unjust discrimination among petrochemical business operators or unjust restrictions on participation in or withdrawal from the concerted acts, as provided in Article 9 (2) 4 of the Act;
7. The anticipated effects on consumers and relevant business operators resulting from the implementation of the concerted acts;
8. The following materials concerning joint research and technological development:
(a) The reasons why joint research and technological development are necessary in the relevant industry and the specific expected effects;
(b) The reasons why it would be difficult for the participating business operators to achieve the objectives through their individual research and technological development efforts.
③ Upon receipt of an application for approval of concerted acts under paragraph (1), the Minister of Trade and Industry shall forward the application and the attached documents under paragraph (2) to the Fair Trade Commission without delay: Provided, That the Minister may return the application if the representative business operator for the concerted acts has entered false or inadequate information in the application for approval of concerted acts or has failed to submit all of the attached documents under paragraph (2).
④ The Fair Trade Commission shall decide whether to give its consent under Article 9 (2) of the Act and send a reply to the Minister of Trade and Industry within 30 days from the date it receives the application for approval of concerted acts forwarded under the main clause of paragraph (3). In such cases, if the application for approval of concerted acts or the attached documents are incomplete, the Fair Trade Commission may specify a period and request the applicant to supplement the documents, and the period from the date the request for supplementation is dispatched to the date the supplementary documents reach the Fair Trade Commission, as well as the period of public notice under paragraph (5), shall not be counted toward the period specified in the first sentence.
⑤ Where the Fair Trade Commission considers it necessary in order to decide whether to give its consent under Article 9 (2) of the Act, it may make the details of the application for approval of concerted acts publicly available for a period not exceeding 30 days and hear the opinions of interested parties.
⑥ The Minister of Trade and Industry shall decide whether to grant approval and notify the applicant of the result within seven days from the date of receipt of the Fair Trade Commission's reply under paragraph (4).

Article 10 (Procedures and Requirements for Information Exchange for Business Restructuring)
① A person intending to engage in the exchange of information for business restructuring pursuant to Article 9 (3) of the Act (hereinafter referred to as "information exchange") shall, no later than 15 days before the scheduled commencement of the information exchange, submit to the Fair Trade Commission an advance notification of information exchange in attached Form No. 2, together with supporting documents showing the purpose and method of the information exchange and the list of information to be exchanged.
② A person exchanging information for business restructuring pursuant to Article 9 (3) of the Act shall comply with the following requirements:
1. Exchange information solely for the purpose of preparing a business restructuring plan or enabling an enterprise approved for business restructuring to pursue business restructuring, including filing a business combination notification;
2. Limit the scope of and participants in information exchange to the minimum necessary, and prohibit information sharing with anyone other than the personnel responsible for information exchange identified in the advance notification;
3. Where competitively sensitive information, such as prices for individual trading partners, must be shared, follow procedures such as replacing real names with anonymous identifiers or aggregating data;
4. Put in place safeguards, such as maintaining an information management register, conducting information exchange through an external institution or an independent dedicated organization, and designating an administrator to supervise and control the flow of information, to ensure that the exchanged information is not used for any purpose other than those specified in subparagraph 1;
5. Where discussions on business restructuring, including a business combination, are discontinued (including where an enterprise that has not been approved for business restructuring does not apply for business restructuring), notify the Fair Trade Commission by submitting a notification of cessation of information exchange in attached Form No. 3, together with documents verifying that the information exchange has ceased, and discard the exchanged information without delay to prevent unfair concerted acts based on the exchanged information.

Article 11 (Reduction of or Exemption from Royalties)
① The following shall be eligible for the reduction of or exemption from royalties under Article 11 (1) 1 of the Act:
1. Enterprises approved for business restructuring;
2. Petrochemical business operators pursuing a transition to high added value.
② The Minister of Trade and Industry may reduce or exempt all or part of the royalties for those specified in the subparagraphs of paragraph (1).
③ Matters concerning the reduction of or exemption from royalties, other than those provided for in paragraphs (1) and (2), shall be as determined and publicly notified by the Minister of Trade and Industry pursuant to Article 15 (4) of the Enforcement Decree of the Industrial Technology Innovation Promotion Act.

Article 12 (Employment Support)
(1) Where the Minister of Trade and Industry deems that an enterprise approved for business restructuring needs employment support under Article 13 (1) of the Act, the Minister may recommend to the Minister of Employment and Labor that the enterprise or its workers be given priority in receiving employment support under statutes and regulations, etc. related to employment support.
(2) Upon receiving a recommendation under paragraph (1), the Minister of Employment and Labor shall give it priority consideration, and the standards and procedures related to the support under paragraph (1) shall be governed by relevant statutes and regulations, etc., including the Employment Insurance Act and the National Lifelong Vocational Skills Development Act.

Article 13 (Compilation of Statistics Related to the Petrochemical Industry)
The scope of statistics related to the petrochemical industry under Article 15 (1) of the Act shall be as follows:
1. Statistics on production, shipments, inventories, capacity utilization rates, and prices by item in the petrochemical industry;
2. Statistics on exports and imports and the trade balance by item and by country in the petrochemical industry;
3. Statistics on the value of production, value added, number of employees, number of business establishments, and amount of investment in plant and equipment in the petrochemical industry;
4. Statistics on production capacity by major country and by product in Korea and abroad, and on the sales of major chemical companies;
5. Statistics on corporate information and financial information of petrochemical companies;
6. Statistics on the status of research and development personnel, research and development expenses, and facility investment expenses in the petrochemical industry;
7. Statistics on energy use and greenhouse gas emissions in the petrochemical industry.

Article 14 (Entrustment of Duties)
(1) The Minister of Trade and Industry may, pursuant to Article 18 (2) of the Act, entrust the duties of compiling and managing statistics on the petrochemical industry under Article 15 (1) of the Act and the duties of surveying and analyzing import trends, etc. under Article 16 (1) of the Act to any of the following institutions or corporations:
1. The Korea Institute for Advancement of Technology under Article 38 of the Industrial Technology Innovation Promotion Act;
2. The Korea Planning and Evaluation Institute of Industrial Technology under Article 39 of the Industrial Technology Innovation Promotion Act;
3. A government-funded research institute established pursuant to the Act on the Establishment, Operation and Fostering of Government-Funded Research Institutes that conducts research related to the petrochemical industry;
4. A corporation related to the petrochemical industry established pursuant to Article 32 of the Civil Act.
(2) Where the Minister of Trade and Industry entrusts duties pursuant to paragraph (1), the Minister shall issue a public notice specifying the institutions or corporations to which duties are entrusted and the details of the entrusted duties.

ADDENDA <No. 36268, April 21, 2026>

Article 1 (Enforcement Date)
This Decree shall enter into force on the date of its promulgation.

Article 2 (Provision on the Enforcement Date of the Special Act on Strengthening the Competitiveness of and Supporting the Petrochemical Industry)
In Article 1 of the Addenda to the Special Act on Strengthening the Competitiveness of and Supporting the Petrochemical Industry (Act No. 21251), "the date prescribed by Presidential Decree" means the date on which this Decree enters into force.
